Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 9   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Finding and Using Degenerate Curves",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["19 October 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Testing Degeneracy", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Defining degeneracy", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Very degenerate curves", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A curve is ",
  StyleBox["degenerate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  if the polynomial representing it factors into homogeneous polynomials \
of ",
  StyleBox["lower degree",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Roughly speaking, the more the polynomial factors the more degenerate the \
curve.  Here is a very useful theorem about when to expect more or less ",
  StyleBox["maximal degeneracy",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A the polynomial for a curve of degree  ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with an ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-tuple point factors into a product of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " linear forms representing lines ",
  StyleBox["concurrent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-tuple point."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " represent the curve and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-tuple point.  By the definition we have \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".    \nNow let ",
  StyleBox["q",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be ",
  StyleBox["any other",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point on the curve.  Since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, q\^n] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", this gives us \[LongDash] by the multilinear properties of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["PD",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \[LongDash] the equation  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, \((a\ p + b\ q)\)\^n] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", for all scalars ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  That means that ",
  StyleBox["all the points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the line \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(h == PE[p, q]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nalso lie on the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".   We need now to invoke here the theorem of algebra allowing us to \
assert that the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["h",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is therefore a  ",
  StyleBox["factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us write ",
  StyleBox["f == g h",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  We then calculate by the product rule: \n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(0 == PD[f, p] == PD[g\ h, p] == g\ PD[h, p] + PD[g, p]\ h == 
          PD[g, p]\ h\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nHence, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[g, p] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We can thus continue ",
  StyleBox["by induction",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to prove the proposition.  (Clearly the basis case of",
  StyleBox[" n == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is obvious.)  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since a bundle of concurrent points cannot intersect in ",
  StyleBox["two distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " unless all the lines degenerate into one line, we can make another \
conclusion as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Corollary.",
  StyleBox["  An ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "n",
  StyleBox["th degree curve with with ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["two distinct",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "n",
  StyleBox["-tuple points is the ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  "n",
  StyleBox["th ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["power",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" of the linear form representing the line joining them.",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  When the polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " factors into linear factors, some of the factors may be the same.  Is \
there any simple way of ",
  StyleBox["(a) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "testing when all the factors are distinct? or ",
  StyleBox["(b) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "finding",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "how many groups of how many equal factors there are?  One would like to \
have a calculation short of doing the actual factorization."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Consider the product of two lines and the point at their intersection; it \
should be a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(l\_1 = a\_1\ x + b\_1\ y + c\_1\ z; \n
    l\_2 = a\_2\ x + b\_2\ y + c\_2\ z; \nf = \ \(l\_1\) l\_2; \n
    f // Tidy\), 
    \(p = PE[l\_1, l\_2]; \np // Tidy\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, p]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is indeed a double point of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Now consider two specific lines:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(l\_1 = x + y + z; \nl\_2 = x - y - z; \nf = l\_1\ l\_2; \nTidy[f]\), 
    \(p = PE[l\_1, l\_2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["Can there be other double points?", "Text"],

Cell["PD[f, a u + b v + c w]//Tidy", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is clear from the coefficients that  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(v\  - \ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " represents the ",
  StyleBox["only",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " double point, ",
  StyleBox["i.e.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[", ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "the only point that will make this result ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Example 3. ",
  StyleBox["Take this ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["cubic",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[":",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = 
      \(-3\)\ x\^3 + 8\ x\^2\ y - 10\ x\ y\^2 + 4\ y\^3 + x\^2\ z + 
        4\ x\ y\ z - 2\ y\^2\ z - 3\ x\ z\^2 + z\^3\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Does it have a ",
  StyleBox["triple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?  Well, It is not so obvious from the look of the polynomial.  So, try to \
factor it."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[f]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So it is partly degenerate.  But does the quadratic factor factor further? \
 Maybe we should throw in ",
  StyleBox["I",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ff = Factor[f, Extension \[Rule] {I}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Yes!  We are lucky with the algebra here.  So, let us find the \
triple point:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[ff\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket], 
      ff\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["That can be simplified, and here is the test:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, u + v + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Can there be another triple point?  Suppose it were ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then the following polynomial would have to be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(der = PD[f, a\ u + b\ v + c\ w] // Tidy\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For that polynomial to be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the coefficients ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have to vanish.  What solutions can there be?  (We have to try twice with \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(c == 0\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], " "}]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", because these are ",
  StyleBox["homogeneous ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "equations.) "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[Join[\(Coefficient[der, #] == 0&\)/@Bxyz[2], {c == 0}], {a, b, c}]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[Join[\(Coefficient[der, #] == 0&\)/@Bxyz[2], {c == 1}], {a, b, c}]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell["The only non-trivial solution is the one we already know.", "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Using the Hessian ", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Defining the Hessian", "Subsubsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Derivatives can be used in many ways.  In particular, there is a lot of \
information  about a curve contained in the ",
  StyleBox["second derivatives",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The totality of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{" ", 
        StyleBox["9",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " second derivatives has a special name."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Definition.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The ",
  StyleBox["Hessian matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(MHP[f]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "of a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is defined as a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(3\[Cross]3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (symmetric) matrix of second derivatives.  The ",
  StyleBox["Hessian determinant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[f]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and it is a polynomial of degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(3\ \((n - 2)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The Hessian for ",
  StyleBox["dual",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["polynomials",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is called ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(MHL[g]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[1, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
Here is the symbolic representations of the two kinds of \
Hessians:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: DegP[f] = 3; \)\), 
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[f]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
ClearAll[g];
g/: DegP[g] = 3;
MatrixForm[MHL[g]]\
\>", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, the program for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["MHP",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is very simple."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?MHP\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The Hessian has many interesting properties for curves of ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " degree.  Here, for the moment, we apply it only to ",
  StyleBox["conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  For such curves, the matrix has only ",
  StyleBox["scalar",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " entries. (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "A ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is degenerate if, and only if,  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[f]] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " For the ",
  StyleBox["necessity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the condition, we let ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " do ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the work."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[a, b, Subscript]; \n
    hes = \ MHP[
        \((a\_1\ x + a\_2\ y + a\_3\ z)\)\ 
          \((b\_1\ x + b\_2\ y + b\_3\ z)\)]; \nhes // MatrixForm\), 
    \(Det[hes]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For the ",
  StyleBox["sufficiency",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", however, we have to invoke some ",
  StyleBox["linear algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The story is somewhat longer.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the conic.  We will prove that in this case ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has a ",
  StyleBox["double point,",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and therefore it factors."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, suppose the determinant ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[f]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " vanishes, then \[LongDash] ",
  StyleBox["as vectors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] the rows of the determinant are linearly dependent.  That \
means we can find multipliers ",
  StyleBox["a, b, c",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] not all zero \[LongDash] such that:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{" ", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["{",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  RowBox[{
                    StyleBox["PD",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["[",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox[\(f, u\^2\),
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox[" ",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["]",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[\(PD[f, u\ v]\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[\(PD[f, u\ w]\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                StyleBox["}",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
            StyleBox["+",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", " ", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["b",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["{",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox[\(PD[f, u\ v]\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    StyleBox["PD",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["[",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox[\(f, v\^2\),
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox[" ",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["]",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[\(PD[f, v\ w]\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
                StyleBox["}",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
            StyleBox["+",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", " ", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["c",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["{",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox[\(PD[f, u\ w]\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[\(PD[f, v\ w]\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  StyleBox[",",
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                  RowBox[{
                    StyleBox["PD",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["[",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox[\(f, w\^2\),
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox[" ",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"], 
                    StyleBox["]",
                      FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
                StyleBox["}",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]}], 
          StyleBox["==",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["0",
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Moving the multipliers inside, we find that the following \
combination also vanishes:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\(\ \ {PD[f, a\ u\^2], PD[f, a\ u\ v], PD[f, a\ u\ w]} + \n
          \ \ {PD[f, b\ u\ v], PD[f, b\ v\^2], PD[f, b\ v\ w]} + \n
          \ \ {PD[f, c\ u\ w], PD[f, c\ v\ w], PD[f, c\ w\^2]} == 0\)\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["The combination on the left can be rewritten as:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[
  "\t{PD[PD[f,a u],u],PD[PD[f,a u],v],PD[PD[f,a u],w]} +\n\t{PD[PD[f,b \
v],u],PD[PD[f,b v],v],PD[PD[f,b v],w]} +\n\t{PD[PD[f,c w],u],PD[PD[f,c \
w],v],PD[PD[f,c w],w]}",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["But this is equal to the single vector:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox[
"\t{PD[PD[f,a u],u] + PD[PD[f,b v],u] + PD[PD[f,c w],u],\n      PD[PD[f,a \
u],v] + PD[PD[f,b v],v] + PD[PD[f,c w],v],\n      PD[PD[f,a u],w] + PD[PD[f,b \
v],w] + PD[PD[f,c w],w]}",
  FontFamily->"Courier",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By ",
  StyleBox["linearity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of polydifferentiation we can then rewrite this as:",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  StyleBox[
  "{PD[PD[f,a u] + PD[f,b v] + PD[f,c w], u], \n\t PD[PD[f,a u] + PD[f,b v] + \
PD[f,c w], v], \n\t PD[PD[f,a u] + PD[f,b v] + PD[f,c w], w]}",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[".\t",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By linearity of polydifferentiation we ",
  StyleBox["further",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " rewrite this as:",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n",
  StyleBox["\t",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  StyleBox["{PD[PD[f,p],u], PD[PD[f,p],v], PD[PD[f,p],w]},",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\nwhere ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(p == a\ u + b\ v + c\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  What we have argued is that this vector ",
  StyleBox["vanishes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "; and, ",
  StyleBox["therefore",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", the linear form",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["PD[f,p]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["also",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " vanishes.   But this shows that",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["p",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  is a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", which is what we needed to show.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
In the case of a square, the Hessian has a very special form.\
\>", 
  "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(MHP[\((a\ x + b\ y + c\ z)\)\^2] // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "All the rows of this matrix are multiples of the vector ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \({a, b, c}\)]],
  ". This is called a ",
  StyleBox["rank",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " matrix.  The rank of the Hessian matrix is an important quantity.  The \
definition can be given in terms of the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(NullSpace\)]],
  " as follows:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(?Rank\)\)], "Input"],

Cell["We find:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Rank[0]\n\), 
    \(Rank[\((a\ x + b\ y + c\ z)\)\^2]\n\), 
    \(Rank[
      \((a\_1\ x + a\_2\ y + a\_3\ z)\)\ \((b\_1\ x + b\_2\ y + b\_3\ z)\)]\n
    \), 
    \(Rank[x\^2 + y\^2 + z\^2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The last result means that the polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^2 + y\^2 + z\^2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot be factored."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Warning.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  In dealing with symbolic coefficients, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " always assumes they are ",
  StyleBox["non",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["trivial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["distinct",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Watch out if you ",
  StyleBox["do not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " want that assumption!!  Note the following results in this regard:"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Rank[a\ x\^2 + b\ y\^2 + c\ z\^2]\n\), 
    \(Rank[a\ x\^2 + b\ y\^2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Actually, the Hessian matrix and determinant of the general conic are both \
rather pleasant algebraically.  Here is a nice way to write the ",
  StyleBox["general quadratic homogeneous polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (or, ",
  StyleBox["symbolic conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ")",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, d, e, f, hes, Subscript]; \n
    con = {a, 2\ b, c, 2\ d, 2\ e, f} . Bxyz[2]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
When we compute the Hessian, we divide out a numerical factor here \
to beautify things:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(hes = MHP[con]\/2; \nMatrixForm[hes]\), 
    \(Det[hes]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The Hessian matrix is is a ",
  StyleBox["symmetric",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " matrix, and the ",
  StyleBox["quadratic form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " it provides is in fact the original polynomial."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Tidy[{x,y,z}.hes.{x,y,z}]", "Input"],

Cell["Here are some specific conics and their Hessians:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Rank[x\^2 - 3\ y\ z]\), 
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[x\^2 - 3\ y\ z]]\), 
    \(Det[%]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Rank[\((x + y + z)\)\^2]\), 
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[\((x + y + z)\)\^2]]\), 
    \(Det[%]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Rank[\((x + y + z)\)\ \((3\ x + 4\ y + 5\ z)\)]\), 
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[\((x + y + z)\)\ \((3\ x + 4\ y + 5\ z)\)]]\), 
    \(Det[%]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Rank[\((x + y + z)\)\ \((3\ x + 4\ y + 5\ z)\) - x\^2]\), 
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[\((x + y + z)\)\ \((3\ x + 4\ y + 5\ z)\) - x\^2]]\), 
    \(Det[%]\)}], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Change of basis", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now the ",
  StyleBox["Hessian matrix",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "was computed with respect to the ",
  StyleBox["standard derivatives",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(\(f /: DegP[f] = 2; \)\), 
    \(MatrixForm[MHP[f]]\)}], "Input",
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["Hessian determinant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is the determinant of this matrix.  What happens under a ",
  StyleBox["change of basis",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ?"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " If the basis ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in the expression ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[MHP[f]]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is subjected to a ",
  StyleBox["linear transformation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then the value of the expression is multiplied by a scalar factor ."
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof  (by example):",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" Consider a representative matrix:",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = {{1, 1, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 4, 9}}; \nDet[M]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can try to calculate the Hessian determinant for a changed \
basis:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f]; \nf /: DegP[f] = 2; \n
    newhesdet = \n\t
      Det[Outer[PD[f, #1\ #2]&, TP[M]/@{u, v, w}, TP[M]/@{u, v, w}]] // Factor
        \)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now let us compare this with the original Hessian determinant:\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[MHP[f]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["transformed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["result",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " was indeed multiplied by ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", which is the ",
  StyleBox["square",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the determinant of the matrix of the transformation.   ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D. (",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["in this example).",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof  (by matrix algebra):",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  We claim that the Hessian matrix under the change of basis by a \
projective transformation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(TP[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " goes over into the matrix product\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(M\  . \ MHP[f]\  . \ Transpose[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  \nThis will be true no matter what degree ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  has.  To start the proof, we take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to be a general matrix:"
}], "Text",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(M = Outer[Subscript, {a, b, c}, {1, 2, 3}]; \n\t\tM // MatrixForm\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us try the calculation with a symbolic polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of degree ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f]; \)\), 
    \(f /: DegP[f] = 6; \n\n
    newhes = Outer[PD[f, #1\ #2]&, TP[M]/@{u, v, w}, TP[M]/@{u, v, w}]; \n\n
    Expand[newhes - M . MHP[f] . Transpose[M]] // MatrixForm\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "(Let us hope that ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  does not lie!   And it will work with higher degree as well.)  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hence, upon taking the determinant, we get the original Hessian \
determinant multiplied by the square of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(Det[M]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?) ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "As a consequence of this result we conclude that the ",
  StyleBox["vanishing",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the Hessian determinant is a ",
  StyleBox["projective invariant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve.  We actually checked this explicitly in the case of ",
  StyleBox["conics",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Why?)"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Finding Tangents", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Tangents and multiple points", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Conic tangents",
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Non-degenerate conics ",
  StyleBox["cannot",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have multiple points, because if they did they would factor into two \
lines.  From our general considerations, it follows at once that if  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  is such a conic and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a point on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the line represented by the linear form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["is in fact the tangent at that point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Why?  Because, if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is any point on the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " other that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then we would have both ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^2]\  == \ 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, p\ q]\  == \ 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " , which ",
  StyleBox["is",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the test for ",
  StyleBox["double contact",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Note that the quantity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f, q\^2]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["cannot vanish",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", since otherwise the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " would lie entirely on the conic \[LongDash] which is ",
  StyleBox["impossible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in the non-degenerate case.  Here are some examples:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "First the ",
  StyleBox["unit circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and the ",
  StyleBox["unit point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["x-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "axis:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p]; \n\(f = x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2; \)\), 
    \(\(p = u + w; \)\), 
    \(PD[f, p\^2]\), 
    \(t = PD[f, p]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["The picture:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, t == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Now, more general points on the ",
  StyleBox["circle",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(p = 
      \((\(-2\) + 2\ a)\)\ u + \((2\ a - a\^2)\)\ v + 
        \((\(-2\) + 2\ a - a\^2)\)\ w; \nExpand[PD[f, p\^2]]\), 
    \(t = Tidy[PD[f, p]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = \(-4\); \n
    ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, t == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 3.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Next take the ",
  StyleBox["parabola",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, f, p, t]; \)\), 
    \(f = x\^2 - y\ z; \np = a\ u + a\^2\ v + w; \nt = PD[f, p]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(a = 1; \n
    ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, t == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-6\), 6}, {y, \(-6\), 6}]; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And we can check that the standard parabola is tangent to the ",
  StyleBox["line at infinity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{v\^2, v}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 4.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Finally, consider the ",
  StyleBox["hyperbola",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text",
  PageBreakAbove->True],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, f, p, t]; \)\), 
    \(f = x\ y - z\^2; \np = a\ u + v\/a + w; \nt = PD[f, p]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(a = 3\/2; \)\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, t == 0} /. {z \[Rule] 1}, \n
      \t{x, \(-6\), 6}, {y, \(-6\), 6}]; \)\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis and the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis appear as ",
  StyleBox["asymptotes",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", but they are actually ",
  StyleBox["tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the hyperbola at infinity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{u\^2, u, v\^2, v}\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The tangents at and from a point", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
We had some special examples of this general theorem about tangents \
earlier.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a curve of degree ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with an ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-tuple point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g == PD[f, p\^\(n - m\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of degree ",
  StyleBox["m ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "factors into ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " linear factors representing ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  the tangents to the curve at the point."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The polynomial ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is of degree ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and is non-zero, and it does factor into ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " linear factors, because \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[g, p] == PD[f, p\^\(n - m + 1\)] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", \nand we can use the theorem on ",
  StyleBox["degenerate curves",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As in the previous proof on degenerate curves, any line with degree of \
contact at least ",
  StyleBox["m+1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " will have all its points on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  So, all the linear forms representing tangents ",
  StyleBox["are",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " factors of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is any point on any of the factors of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then \n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[f, p\^\(n - i\)\ q\^i] == PD[g, p\^\(m - i\)\ q\^i] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " \nholds for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["i",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["m",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, all these lines have a degree of contact with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " of at least ",
  StyleBox["m+1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and so they are ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " tangents. ",
  StyleBox[" Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  This theorem shows that at an ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-tuple point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " there are ",
  StyleBox["m",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " tangents \[LongDash] but some of them may be counted ",
  StyleBox["multiple times",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " depending on how ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p\^\(n - m\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " factors."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 1.   ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "The following ",
  StyleBox["cubic polynomial",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " does not factor, and so the curve is not degenerate."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f]; f = x\^3 + y\^2\ z + x\ y\ z; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "  is a double point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, w\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here are the two tangents at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(tt = Factor[PD[f, w]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Here is the picture of the configuration.  (A large number of \
points have to be plotted in order to get a smoth picture of the curve.)\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, 
          tt\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0, 
          tt\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket] == 0} /. {
          z \[Rule] 1}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] False, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 200]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
By the way the tangent at the point at infinity can be found as \
follows:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, v\^3], PD[f, v\^2]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Could the cubic have ",
  StyleBox["another",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " double point?  Check the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["PD",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " for an arbitrary point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \)\), 
    \(coord = ExpandAll[CL[PD[f, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^2]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Can these coordinates ",
  StyleBox["all vanish",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for any other point than ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "?  We need only try with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["c",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "either ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[\((coord /. c \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, {a, b}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The single solution here corresponds to the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[\((coord /. c \[Rule] 0)\) == 0, {a, b}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The only solutions correspond to the improper point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Therefore, there is only one double point."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Of course, we can argue in general that a ",
  StyleBox["cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "can have only one double point.  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " were ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " distinct double points, then the line joining them would intersect the \
cubic ",
  StyleBox["four times",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  But this is impossible!"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Example 2.   ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Next is an example of a ",
  StyleBox["7",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree curve with a ",
  StyleBox["triple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " where two tangents have collapsed to one."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, p]; p = w; \), 
    \(f = x\^7 - x\ y\^2\ z\^4 - y\^3\ z\^4; \n
    \((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{p\^7, p\^6, p\^5, p\^4, p\^3}\), 
    \(Factor[%\[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((f /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0, y == 0, x + y == 0}, {x, 
        \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] False]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ah,ha, since the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis is counted twice, it has a very high degree of contact.  Are there \
other multiple points?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a, b, c]; \)\), 
    \(dir = Expand[PD[f, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^5]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(coef = Union[Flatten[CoefficientList[dir, {x, y, z}]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Can these coefficients  ",
  StyleBox["all vanish",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for any other point than ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(w\)]],
  "?  We need only try with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(coef /. c \[Rule] 1\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is obvious for  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we must have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == b == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now we try ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(coef /. c \[Rule] 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Clearly ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is necessary but ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["indeterminant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Well!  Maybe ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a triple point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@{v\^7, v\^6, v\^5, v\^4, v\^3}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ah ha!  The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["triple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " but ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is even ",
  StyleBox["quadruple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "\[LongDash]which is possible for degree ",
  StyleBox["7",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Note that there is only one tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\[LongDash]but it is counted ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " times.  Can we \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]see\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] the \
point and the tangent?  Well, make the projective transformation that \
interchanges ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((f /. {y \[Rule] 1, z \[Rule] y})\) == 0, y == 0}, {
        x, \(-2\), 2}, Axes \[Rule] False]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Oh, well, the picture is OK but not exciting.  ", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We turn to a theorem about",
  StyleBox[" counting tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "is a curve of degree",
  StyleBox[" n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is  ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " an ",
  StyleBox["n-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "tuple point of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the non-zero curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, \ p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "of degree",
  StyleBox[" n-1 ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "intersects ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " exactly at multiple points and points of contact of the tangents to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " from the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Hence, there can be ",
  StyleBox["at most",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(n \((n - 1)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " tangents to the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " from the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "be a multiple point of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Then we have certainly ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, q\^\(n - 1\)] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But then\n\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[PD[f, p], q\^\(n - 1\)] == PD[PD[f, q\^\(n - 1\)], p] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nTherefore, any multiple point of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on ",
  StyleBox["PD[f,p]",
    FontFamily->"Courier",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  \nNext, let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be a point lying on the two curves which is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " a multiple point of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, q\^\(n - 1\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is in fact the tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  But, since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, p]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", by assumption, then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " clearly lies on this tangent.   ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " This theorem also gives a (very rough) upper bound on the ",
  StyleBox["number of multiple points ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "of a non-degenerate curve."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Higher-degree examples", "Subsection"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData["The Valentine\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Day curve"], "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider the following ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-segree curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[\((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^3 == 2\ x\^2\ y\^3, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
      Axes \[Rule] None, PlotStyle \[Rule] Red]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
It is easier to see some of the points on the curve with the axes \
drawn in.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[\((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^3 == 2\ x\^2\ y\^3, \n
      \t{x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the homogeneous polynomial for the curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(h = \((x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2)\)\^3 - 2\ x\^2\ y\^3\ z; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us check the unit points on the axes.  ",
  StyleBox["(Note the trick with the exponents.)",
    Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[h, #1]&)\)/@\(\((v + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(v + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is thus a ",
  StyleBox["doube point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with a vertical ",
  StyleBox["double tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[h, #1]&)\)/@\(\((\(-v\) + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-v\)\  + \ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["is ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["similar",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" to ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(v\  + \ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u\  + \ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is quite different, however."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[h, #1]&)\)/@\(\((u + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4, 3}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have found a ",
  StyleBox["triple point!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The shape (of the real part) of the curve surely does not suggest this \
possibility.  We try to ",
  StyleBox["factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the cubic derivative which ought to represent the tangents."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(tt = Factor[Last[%], Extension \[Rule] {2\^\(1/3\)}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The factorization is partly successful, and we can see the one \
tangent.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^3 == 2\ x\^2\ y\^3, 
        \(-2\)\ x + 2\^\(1/3\)\ y == \(-2\)}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, 
      \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 200, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] {Red, Black}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Now, let us factor the other quadratic part by intersecting a \
horizontal line to find two points.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ll = Last[tt]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{ll == 0, y == 1, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(pp = u\ x + v\ y + w\ z /. %\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ff = \((PE[u + w, #1]&)\)/@pp\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[4\ Times@@ff]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, we have found the two linear factors.  The product is a real \
polynomial, but the factors are complex.  What this probably means is that \
there are two branches going through ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " which we cannot see because the points on them need complex coordinates. \
"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " takes ",
  StyleBox["quite a long time",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to do the factorization."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[
        4\ x\^2 + 2\ 2\^\(1/3\)\ x\ y + 2\^\(2/3\)\ y\^2 - 8\ x\ z - 
          2\ 2\^\(1/3\)\ y\ z + 4\ z\^2, 
        Extension \[Rule] {2\^\(1/3\), I, \@3}] // Timing\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can look at a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]nearby\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
point to ",
  StyleBox["u+w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a]; \)\), 
    \(rr = 
      Solve[PD[h, \((\((1 + 1\/10)\)\ u + a\ v + w)\)\^6] == 0, {a}]\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since finding it required solving a  ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree equation, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " had to use a rather \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]generic\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] notation for the roots \
of the polynomial.  There are, however, two real roots, as we can see ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a numerical approximation."
}], "Text"],

Cell["rr // N", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Let us look at the first of them and test whether the point is on \
the curve.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(a = a /. First[rr]; \)\), 
    \(Simplify[Together[PD[h, \((\((1 + 1\/10)\)\ u + a\ v + w)\)\^6]]]\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is.  But is it a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[Together[PD[h, \((\((1 + 1\/10)\)\ u + a\ v + w)\)\^5]]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["The algebra is too hard, so take an approximation again.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ll = N[Expand[%]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It seems safe to say this is not ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", and indeed it is a tangent to the curve.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^3 == 2\ x\^2\ y\^3, 
        \((ll /. z \[Rule] 1)\) == 0}, {x, \(-2\), 2}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotStyle \[Rule] {Red, Black}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So the following point is just a ",
  StyleBox["simple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[\((1 + 1\/10)\)\ u + a\ v + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It might be interesting to find the real ",
  StyleBox["infletion points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve.  "
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Breaking hearts", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
The kind of heart-shaped curve we just saw is just one curve a \
related family of curves.  We experiment with graphing several families.\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Do[
      ImplicitPlot[
        \((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^3 == x\^i, {x, \(-2\), 3}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, 
        PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}], {i, 0, 5}]; 
    \)\)], "Input",
  AnimationCycleOffset->1,
  AnimationCycleRepetitions->Infinity],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the ",
  StyleBox["second curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of this set. We can check to see that it has an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point ",
  StyleBox["v + w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = \((x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2)\)\^3 - x\ z\^5\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@\(\((v + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the ",
  StyleBox["last curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the family.  It has a ",
  StyleBox["triple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point ",
  StyleBox["v + w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " which has a horizontal triplr tangent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g = \((x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2)\)\^3 - x\^5\ z\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[g, #1]&)\)/@\(\((v + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4, 3}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Last[%]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The next family mixes ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Do[
      ImplicitPlot[\((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^3 == 2\ x\^2\ y\^i, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-2\), 3}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
        AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}], {i, 0, 5}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the second curve of this set. It is also seen to have an ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point ",
  StyleBox["u + w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = \((x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2)\)\^3 - 2\ x\^2\ y\ z\^3\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@\(\((u + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the next to last curve of the family.  It has a ",
  StyleBox["triple point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " at the point ",
  StyleBox["v + w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " which has a horizontal triple tangent."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g = \((x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2)\)\^3 - 2\ x\^2\ y\^4\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[g, #1]&)\)/@\(\((v + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[g, #1]&)\)/@\(\((u + w)\)\^{6, 5, 4, 3}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Last[%]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The next family mixes ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Do[
      ImplicitPlot[\((x\^2 + y\^2 - 1)\)\^4 == 2\ x\^2\ y\^i, \n
        \t\t{x, \(-2\), 3}, {y, \(-2\), 2}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 200, 
        AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}], {i, 0, 5}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us check out the ",
  StyleBox["last",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curves."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f = \((x\^2 + y\^2 - z\^2)\)\^4 - 2\ x\^2\ y\^5\ z\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There is a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@\(\((v + w)\)\^{8, 7, 6}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And there is a ",
  StyleBox["quadruple",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " point with vertical double tangents."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@\(\((u + w)\)\^{8, 7, 6, 5, 4}\)\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Last[%]]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
